    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-g/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Setting hypotheses: Let ρ be the population product moment correlation coefficient. [M1] Enter each town as a pair, with adverts and sales occupying the two columns of the same row: Test H0 : ρ = 0 [A1] against H1 : ρ̸ = 0. The ten pairs give r ≈0.8166. [A1] The two-tailed 10% critical magnitude is 0.5494. [A1] Since 0.8166 &gt; 0.5494, reject H0. [A1] There is evidence of positive correlation between the number of advertisements and sales: [A1] The association alone does not establish that advertising caused the extra sales . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:656.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 7 marks.</p>"
